    "prompt": "The “rose” shown is formed from two kinds of rhombuses, the thin ones coloured dark gray and the big ones coloured either white or light gray. What is the largest angle of a big rhombus?",
    "image": "qimages/kangaroo/ca-2023-g0708/q28.webp",
    "choices": [
      {
        "key": "A",
        "text": "106°"
      },
      {
        "key": "B",
        "text": "108°"
      },
      {
        "key": "C",
        "text": "110°"
      },
      {
        "key": "D",
        "text": "112°"
      },
      {
        "key": "E",
        "text": "120°"
      }
    ],
    "answer": "B",
    "acceptedAnswers": null,
    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "Angles around a point",
        "body": "In the centre 10 thin rhombuses meet, so each has an angle of $360° \\div 10 = 36°$ there; its other angles are 144°, 36° and 144°.\n\nLook at the end of a side shared by two neighbouring thin rhombuses (away from the centre). Both thin rhombuses have a 144° angle there, which leaves $360° - 2\\cdot144° = 72°$ for the big white rhombus that fills the gap. A rhombus with a 72° angle has angles 72° and $180° - 72° = 108°$.\n\n**Answer: (B) 108°**"
      }
    ],
